// src/v4/std/nat.dag
//
// Scope: natural numbers — the Peano-inductive non-negative integers; the
//        base of the numeric tower (integer widths and code points build
//        on it).
// Anchor: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Natural_number (Peano axioms)
//
// Owns:
//   - Nat — Peano inductive: Zero | Succ { prev: Nat } (a recursive `Disj`,
//     recursion via Node children per A1 — not a self-referential type)
//   - inhabitance: Nat inhabits `Monoid` (under +) / `Semiring`
//     (std/algebra.dag) — arithmetic falls out of the algebra
//
// Consumes:
//   - std/node.dag: Disj
//   - std/algebra.dag: Monoid, Semiring
//
// Status: T-3 modeled 2026-05-16 (vivid-carp-663). Replaces part of the
//   deleted std/primitive.dag; see src/v4/STRUCTURE.md §"scalar/numeric
//   concept decomposition".
// Brief: see BRIEF_TEMPLATE.md; this file's I/O contract is the immutable
// contract for the worker. Splitting this file requires explicit operator
// ratification (substrate extension = stop signal).


module v4.std.nat


// Constructing a `Monoid`/`Semiring` instance-value names its whole
// refinement chain — `Monoid` nests `Semigroup` nests `Magma`, and
// `Semiring.add` is a `CommutativeMonoid` (algebra.dag's "refinement is
// structural nesting" decision). The scaffold header's "Consumes:
// Monoid, Semiring" names the conceptual grounding; the nested carriers
// are part of those structures' own definitions, so importing them is
// the mechanical consequence of the inhabitance form, not a scope
// expansion.
import v4.std.algebra { Magma, Semigroup, Monoid, CommutativeMonoid, Semiring }


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Nat — the Peano natural numbers.
//
// Inductive definition (Peano 1889): a natural number is either `Zero`,
// or the `Succ`essor of a natural number. `Succ` carries its predecessor,
// so the value `Succ { prev: Succ { prev: Zero } }` IS the number two —
// the representation is unary and structural, the count of `Succ` layers.
//
// Recursion lives in the `prev` child (node.dag A1: the one recursive
// type is `Node`; a recursive `Disj` like this is a finite Node tree
// whose recursive occurrence is a name-reference resolved through the
// namespace — `Nat` referencing its own declaration — never a
// self-referential type slot). The recursion is REGULAR (the recursive
// occurrence `prev: Nat` is `Nat` itself, not a different instantiation),
// so it is unconditionally admissible per A1. Descent on `prev` is the
// A2 IMPLICIT termination case: every recursion over a Nat strips one
// `Succ`, a proper sub-Node, so it is total by construction with no
// ranking measure needed.
//
// Anchor: Peano axioms — https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Natural_number.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: Zero/Succ are not per-consumer facts;
//      every structural fold over a Nat handles BOTH (induction base +
//      successor step). No consumer reads only one variant.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: the variants have DIFFERENT shapes
//      (Zero nullary; Succ {prev: Nat}). Collapsing to a uniform record
//      {kind, prev?} makes "Succ with no prev" representable — the
//      inverse of P2 (illegal states unrepresentable).
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A by construction: Nat is a ROOT of the
//      numeric tower (integer widths and code points build on it —
//      STRUCTURE.md §"scalar/numeric concept decomposition"). The
//      algebras it inhabits below (Monoid, Semiring) are structures
//      imposed ON Nat — their carrier IS Nat — not a richer source Nat
//      decomposes into; pointing Nat's variants at them is circular.
//      Peano induction is the irreducible source, not a compression.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a Nat is EITHER zero OR a successor;
//      genuine alternatives (induction base vs inductive step), never
//      simultaneous coordinates. No value is "both".
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: Zero (nullary) and Succ
//      ({prev: Nat}) are structurally heterogeneous — NOT one generic
//      `F<X>` / projection over a separately-declared source set
//      (Practice 7). They are the two constructors of an inductive
//      type, not an enumerated copy of a set.
// Terminal: the irreducible Peano-inductive definition of the natural
// numbers; the base of the numeric tower.
type Nat
  = Zero
  | Succ { prev: Nat }


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// nat_add — addition on Nat, the Peano recursive definition:
//   0 + b          = b                  (Zero arm)
//   Succ(a') + b   = Succ(a' + b)       (Succ arm)
// Single self-recursive; the recursion descends on the `prev` sub-Node
// of `a` (node.dag A2 IMPLICIT termination — total by construction, no
// ranking measure needed). This IS the `op` of `nat_additive_monoid`
// below: arithmetic is not re-declared, it falls out of the algebra
// instance-value (epistemic stacking).
fn nat_add(a: Nat, b: Nat) -> Nat {
  match a {
    Zero => b
    Succ { prev: p } => Succ { prev: nat_add(a: p, b: b) }
  }
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// nat_mul — multiplication on Nat, the Peano recursive definition:
//   0 * b          = 0
//   Succ(a') * b   = b + (a' * b)
// Single self-recursive; the recursion descends on the `prev` sub-Node
// of `a`. It composes `nat_add` (the additive op) — `mul` distributing
// over `add` is the semiring's defining axiom, here a grounded property
// of the definition rather than a stored flag. This IS the `op` of the
// multiplicative monoid in `nat_semiring` below.
fn nat_mul(a: Nat, b: Nat) -> Nat {
  match a {
    Zero => Zero
    Succ { prev: p } => nat_add(a: b, b: nat_mul(a: p, b: b))
  }
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Inhabitance — Nat grounds in the algebra structures of std/algebra.dag.
//
// Per the operator-ratified inhabitance discipline, inhabitance IS a
// `data <name>: <Algebra><Carrier> = <Algebra> { … }` instance-value
// declared HERE, in the inhabiting type's own file — never a carrier
// field, never an `inhabits` keyword. The arithmetic operations fall
// out by walking these instances; nothing is re-declared.

// nat_additive_monoid — (Nat, +, 0) is a monoid.
// `op = nat_add` is associative and `Zero` is its two-sided identity
// (nat_add(Zero, b) == b definitionally; nat_add(a, Zero) == a by
// induction on a). These identity/associativity axioms ARE the
// definition of `Monoid` (algebra.dag) — grounded, not stored flags.
// Addition is also commutative, so (Nat, +, 0) is in fact a commutative
// monoid; that strengthening is carried structurally by the
// `CommutativeMonoid` wrapper at `nat_semiring.add` below.
// Anchor: Peano arithmetic / monoid theory (Wikipedia "Monoid").
data nat_additive_monoid: Monoid<Nat> = Monoid {
  semigroup: Semigroup { magma: Magma { op: nat_add } },
  identity: Zero
}

// nat_semiring — (Nat, +, ×, 0, 1) is a semiring.
// `add` is the additive `CommutativeMonoid` built directly on
// `nat_additive_monoid` — single authority for the additive structure
// (INVARIANTS P2: the additive monoid is declared once and referenced,
// not re-listed). `mul` is the multiplicative `Monoid` with `op =
// nat_mul` and identity one = `Succ { prev: Zero }`. `nat_mul`
// distributing over `nat_add` and `Zero` annihilating are the defining
// semiring axioms — grounded in the definition of `Semiring`
// (algebra.dag), never stored as fields or flags.
// Anchor: semiring theory (Wikipedia "Semiring").
data nat_semiring: Semiring<Nat> = Semiring {
  add: CommutativeMonoid { monoid: nat_additive_monoid },
  mul: Monoid {
    semigroup: Semigroup { magma: Magma { op: nat_mul } },
    identity: Succ { prev: Zero }
  }
}
